AB  - Room acoustics significantly influence learning outcomes in both traditional (in-person) and distance education environments. Artefacts such as background noise and reverberation can negatively affect students' attention, memory, and comprehension. With the growth of distance learning and immersive technologies such as virtual and augmented reality, the importance of high-quality audio becomes even more critical. A possible way to address acoustic interference is through denoising and speech separation. A central component in addressing reverberations is room impulse response. It contains information about sound reflections and material properties making it an essential asset in classroom design, speech transmission improvement, and immersive simulations. This paper reviews applications and research works in various educational contexts, from acoustic treatment of classrooms to speech quality in distance learning and usage in diverse virtual reality simulations. Furthermore, this work reviews different methods for obtaining RIR, ranging from classical measurement techniques to modern machine learning approaches. Finally, the paper outlines opportunities for integrating acoustic modelling into educational practices to improve their effectiveness, quality, and accessibility.
