AB  - Generative AI-assisted writing often requires post-generation editing. In a dynamic mobile environment, where user attention is divided, a key challenge is to reduce the cognitive load of such generate-edit cycle. To address the challenge, we propose a "macro-to-micro" control approach to balance user agency and automation in the AI-assisted writing process. This approach offers distinct interaction methods for macro-level adjustments (e.g., tone) and micro-level modifications (e.g., words). We conducted two user studies to evaluate its effectiveness in mobile text editing. Our preliminary results show that our approach significantly reduces cognitive load in demanding mobile situations. We identified a "control paradox": users felt more confident and preferred our approach, despite perceiving less direct operational control. Furthermore, our second study identified an important modality mismatch: the voice-centric modality for the micro-editing layer was a significant bottleneck. This finding suggests a key design principle for future systems: effective control is not about more or less, but about matching the interaction modality to the task’s required precision.
ER  - 

