AB  - The article aims to understand the transformation of the existential notion of death within the digital reality and the generative AI’s input in the process; the study focuses on the phenomenon of “deathbots” as a specific way of the deceased’s continued existence within the digital reality. Theoretical basis of the paper is resting on Jean-Paul Sartre’s and Martin Heidegger’s definitions of death as an existential notion and the digital humanities research of what’s known as “digital afterlife”, i.e. the continued existence of an individual in the digital reality even after the physical passing, utilizing a combination of existential analysis, death studies methods, and elements of content analysis. The article seeks to understand the existential implications of deathbots as avatars of the decedent through notions of memory and presence, how they’re relevant to retaining the image and preserving the existence of an individual after the passing; existential relevance of these notions is further uncovered and pondered. The conclusion provides analysis to the existential and thanatological consequences of “deathbots”: blurring of the line between life and death, illusion of the “response from the beyond”, alterations of the grieving processes, as well as potential rejection of death as an ontological finality.
ER  - 

